→ `marker_user_sid`)sandbox-user-sidRead `{denyRead:[…], denyWrite:[…]}` from stdin and add an additive `(D;OICI;mask;;;<sid>)` ACE for the sandbox user on each target plus a `(D;OICI;FILE_DELETE_CHILD;;;<sid>)` on the parent — NO PROTECTED rewrite, no SD snapshot. Refcounted per holder; `acl restore` removes the ACE when the last holder releases. Globs are rejected; directory targets get an `(OI)(CI)` inheriting ACE covering the subtreeHolder PID (see `acl stamp`)SID to grant — the dedicated sandbox user (`srt-win user status` → `marker_user_sid`)PERSISTENTSession start for `exec --write-restricted-token`. Each `write` path becomes a persistent root: it gets a capability SID of its own and ACEs for the sandbox GROUP and that SID (none for the sandbox user), which are KEPT when the session ends — `acl revoke` does not touch them, `acl forget` removes them. A root already granted costs one DACL read. stdin may also carry `denyRead` / `denyWrite`: targets under a persistent root are stamped for the sandbox group and kept with it; the others, and the `read` paths, are per-session as without this flag. Pass each root to `exec` as `--write-root`Session start for `exec --write-restricted-token`. Each `write` path becomes a persistent root: it gets a capability SID of its own and ACEs for the sandbox GROUP and that SID (none for the sandbox user), which are KEPT when the session ends — `acl revoke` does not touch them, `acl forget` removes them. A root already granted costs one DACL read. stdin may also carry `denyRead` / `denyWrite`: targets under a persistent root are stamped for the sandbox group and kept with it; the others, and the `read` paths, are per-session as without this flag. Pass each root to `exec` as `--write-root`.

Exits **15** unless the install's setup marker is this build's: no grant is kept on a machine where a build of another setup version would still launch the sandbox account.ALLOW_PROFILE_EXCLUSIONWhile a profile read grant is recorded, no path is granted here that is a protected exclusion of it, lies inside one, or lies above one, nor one that is or lies above a directory it leaves ungranted as a whole (the temporary directory): the command fails and grants nothing. This flag names a `read` path that may be granted although it is a protected exclusion or lies inside one. Repeatable. It admits no `write` path, and no path above an exclusionallow-profile-exclusionRead `{read:[…], write:[…]}` from stdin and add an inheritable `(OI)(CI)` ALLOW ACE for `--sandbox-user-sid` on each path (`FILE_GENERIC_READ|EXECUTE` for `read`, `MODIFY_NO_FDC` for `write`). Additive — the path's existing DACL and inheritance are untouched. Refcounted per holder; `acl revoke` removes the ACE when the last holder releasesRead `{read:[…], write:[…]}` from stdin and add an inheritable `(OI)(CI)` ALLOW ACE for `--sandbox-user-sid` on each path (`FILE_GENERIC_READ|EXECUTE` for `read`, `MODIFY_NO_FDC` for `write`). Additive — the path's existing DACL and inheritance are untouched. Refcounted per holder; `acl revoke` removes the ACE when the last holder releases.

Refused while a persistent grant exists (see `--persistent`); `acl forget --all` removes those.RevokeEmit a single JSON array of per-path `{path, status}` objects on stdout. The array is complete — it includes the paths that could not be released — and is written before a non-zero exitDrop the holder's claim on every granted path; remove the sandbox-user ACE on any path whose refcount falls to zeroDrop the holder's claim on every granted path; remove the sandbox-user ACE on any path whose refcount falls to zero.

Exits non-zero when any grant could not be released (status `unreadable` or `failed`: the ACE may still be on disk and its rows are kept for a retry). A caller that goes on to `acl restore` must not do so in that case.RestoreHolder PID whose stamps to release (see `acl stamp`). Must match the value passed at stamp timeThe sandbox user's SID — must match `acl stamp`Emit a single JSON array of per-path `{path, status}` objects on stdout (exit 0 always); the host raises any error AFTER reading the array. Without this flag, the existing human-readable summary goes to stderrDrop the holder's claim on every DENY ACE it stamped; remove the sandbox-user ACE on any path whose refcount falls to zeroALLEvery root recorded for the calling userEmit a JSON array of per-root `{path, status}` objects on stdout (`removed`, `absent`, `failed`, `dropped`), also when exiting non-zeroWith `--all` only: remove nothing, print `{"held_by": [{pid, kind, path}]}` on stdout and exit 22, if a live session other than the one of this process id is running on any kept write root. (Not offered for named roots: a hold is recorded by path, a root is forgotten by the object it is, and the two would have to be matched.)DROP_PENDINGStop waiting for the volume of a grant that `forget` reported as pending, and drop its record: for a volume that will not come back (reformatted, discarded). The entries on that object are NOT removed — it cannot be reached — and nothing will remove them later; the command says so, with the group they name. Nothing else ever drops a pending record. Repeatable; an unknown path is an errordrop-pendingWith `--all`: a root whose object can no longer be located (its drive is gone, say) is reported and its record dropped instead of failing the command. Its ACEs may still be on diskROOTSThe roots to forgetRemove persistent write grants (`acl grant --persistent`): the root's ACEs, the deny ACEs kept with it, its capability SID and its record. One walk of the tree per root. Exits non-zero if any root could not be completed; that root stays recordedRecoverDrop the record of a dead holder's grant that can no longer be located (its path is gone and its file id cannot be looked up), then release that holder's deny ACEs. Each dropped path is printed; its ACE may still be on diskEmit `{"deadBrokers": N, "acesRevoked": N}` on stdout. (Not the per-path array — recover sweeps by trustee SID and does not enumerate paths.)Run crash-recovery only: release the holds of dead holders (grants first; a holder's deny ACEs are kept while one of its grants could not be released) and prune them. Exits non-zero when a grant is still pendingProfileBusyPrint WFP fence state as JSON: `{state, filters, port_range?, allow_loopback, loopback_notice?, user_sid?, hint?}`. `allow_loopback` is whether the installed set opens loopback (read off the filters when elevated, off the install record otherwise); when it does, `loopback_notice` says in plain words what that exposes. BFE's `FwpmFilterCreateEnumHandle0` is admin-gated, so a non-elevated caller gets `{state:"cannot-read", hint:…}` (exit 0). The non-elevated readiness check is `wfp verify`Print WFP fence state as JSON: `{state, filters, port_range?, allow_loopback, loopback_notice?, user_sid?, hint?}`. `allow_loopback` is whether the installed set opens loopback (read off the filters when elevated, off the install record otherwise); when it does, `loopback_notice` says in plain words what that exposes. BFE's `FwpmFilterCreateEnumHandle0` is admin-gated, so a non-elevated caller gets `{state:"cannot-read", hint:…}` (exit 0). The non-elevated readiness check is `wfp verify`.

`state:"loopback-mismatch"` (with a `hint`): the loopback mode of the filters is not the one the install recorded. Only an elevated caller can see it.VerifyProbe target (`host:port`). The host binds a local listener on an ephemeral loopback port OUTSIDE the WFP permit range and passes it here, so the fence-inactive case is deterministic without depending on any external host. With `--allow-loopback` it is the listener `open_loopback_probe` must reach, and must be a loopback address (exit 1 otherwise)This session expects loopback open. Compared with the installed mode (exit 19 on a difference in either direction) and, when given, probedOff-loopback target (`host:port`) for the attempt made while impersonating the anonymous token, and for the lowered-integrity attempts' off-loopback half. Default: this machine's own outbound address at `target`'s port — nothing needs to listen there, since a refused connect shows the filters let it through just as a completed one does. With no such address the attempt is skippedAlso make the off-loopback attempt under the anonymous token (`anonymous_probe`, residual `anonymous-egress`). No filter refuses it, so it never changes the exit code, and it can wait on its destination; without this flag it is `skipped`Probe with the token `exec --write-restricted-token` gives the child, instead of the default one. The connects are then made by a lockdown child (`srt-win probe egress`) holding that token, because the WFP block filter matches by an access check of the connecting token and a restricted token is checked differently from the runner's. A host that runs commands with `--write-restricted-token` must verify with this flagThe session's write roots, as given to `exec`: the probe child's token then carries the same capability SIDsBehavioral egress-block probe — the non-elevated readiness check for the WFP fence. Spawns the runner as `srt-sandbox` (via `CreateProcessWithLogonW`), which attempts direct TCP connects under each token shape a sandboxed process can present (`srt_win::probe`)Behavioral egress-block probe — the non-elevated readiness check for the WFP fence. Spawns the runner as `srt-sandbox` (via `CreateProcessWithLogonW`), which attempts direct TCP connects under each token shape a sandboxed process can present (`srt_win::probe`).

Prints one JSON line. Each `*_probe` is `blocked`, `connected`, `refused`, `unreachable`, `skipped` or `error`:

- `egress_probe` — own token, to `target`. - `anonymous_probe` — impersonating the anonymous token, to `anonymous_target`. - `anonymous_loopback_probe` — impersonating the anonymous token, to `target`. - `deny_only_probe` — a copy of the own token with the user SID deny-only, to `target`. - `low_integrity_probe`, `low_integrity_loopback_probe` — a copy of the own token at low integrity, to `anonymous_target` and to `target`. - `untrusted_integrity_probe`, `untrusted_integrity_loopback_probe` — the same at untrusted integrity. - `open_loopback_probe` — own token, to `target`, only with `--allow-loopback` (else `skipped`): it must connect. - `residuals` — names of the gaps `anonymous_probe`, `anonymous_loopback_probe` and `deny_only_probe` found open (`anonymous-egress`, `anonymous-loopback`, `deny-only-user-sid`). The filters do not close these; they are reported, with a warning on stderr, and do not change the exit code. `anonymous-egress` is expected wherever the machine has an off-loopback address: no filter is keyed on the anonymous token. `lowered-integrity-unprobed` is named when a lowered-integrity probe is `error`: that token shape could not be set up, so no connect under it was observed.

With `--allow-loopback` one loopback port stays blocked for the sandbox account (`wfp::LOOPBACK_PROBE_PORT`), and nothing listens on it. `egress_probe` and the lowered-integrity loopback probes go there (`egress_target`) and pass only on the filter's refusal: for `egress_probe`, a refusal by the stack, a connect or no answer in time all mean the block did not fire (exit 3). `anonymous_loopback_probe` and `deny_only_probe` are `skipped`.

Every probe but `egress_probe` and `open_loopback_probe` (both run under the runner's own token) makes its connect twice: once on a socket created before the token is assumed and once on a socket created after, since the engine attributes a connect to the token in effect when its socket was created. The JSON carries the worse of the two results for the fence; stderr carries one line per ordering, each naming it (`socket before token`, `socket after token`).

Every other probe but `open_loopback_probe` must be blocked: `egress_probe` and the four lowered-integrity ones, whose token still carries the sandbox account's SID. Exits **0** (`egress_probe` was blocked; each lowered-integrity one was blocked, skipped for want of an off-loopback address, or `error`; and with `--allow-loopback` `open_loopback_probe` connected = fence active), **3** (one got past t